{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_9","type":"agent_message","text":"1. **Consensus requirements pass.** Entries remain intact; resolver functions are invoked synchronously and never retained. The sweep preserves order and threads cursor updates—including gap recovery—before resolving later entries. Only reader `blocked` and writer `full` wait; writer `closed` remains terminal. I verified `src/cljc/dao/stream.cljc` is byte-identical to `18664048`.\n\n2. **Invalid-answer handling matches the documented revision.** Validation distinguishes malformed answers from qualified undeclared outcomes. Malformed answers and poll-time throws become terminal `invalid-answer` diagnostics without cursor advancement. Resolver calls remain outside those catches, so resolver throws escape exactly as documented. “Voids the sweep” means no result returns; it does **not** undo earlier appends, and the documentation correctly warns against replaying the previous waitset.\n\n3. **The unchanged-suite requirement remains sound for W2.** Stricter validation changes defective-answer behavior, not valid stream semantics. The inspected VM reader fixtures return conforming answers or delegate to existing transports. W2 must still prove compatibility after integration and route all waitset diagnostics through its error path before restoration; W1’s successful suites do not substitute for that gate.\n\nThe tests cover the core architectural laws and pin advancement counts. I found no blocking defect. This was a read-only audit; I relied on the supplied execution results rather than rerunning the suites.\n\nSIGN-OFF: granted — W1 satisfies the agreed sweep contract and documented diagnostic behavior, with unchanged-suite verification correctly remaining a W2 integration gate."}}
